 "productionNotes": "Volvo's own numbers are the most precise on offer and they are internally consistent. Volvo Car USA's 2013 press release, marking twenty years since the end of production, states that 2,862,573 cars were built over nineteen years, of which 177,402 were 260s. The 2014 international release gives the split directly: 2,685,171 240s and 177,402 260s. The heritage pages on volvocars.com carry a per-body count that adds up to the same 240 figure: 1,483,399 four-door 244s, 959,151 five-door 245s and 242,621 two-door 242s, the last of those running only from 1974 to 1984. Matthew's Volvo Site repeats the same three body totals. The disagreement is with the secondary literature. Hagerty's buyer's guide says there were over 2.8 million models built using the 240 designation, plus a further 177,000 nearly identical 260s, which treats the combined 240/260 total as the 240 figure alone. Wikipedia and Matthew's Volvo Site both round to 2.8 million without saying whether the 260 is included. Because a manufacturer figure of 2,685,171 and a widely repeated 2.8 million-plus figure for the 240 alone are both in circulation, no single total is asserted here; the difference is the 177,402 260-series cars. No source consulted here publishes a US-only production or import total for the 240. What exists instead is calendar-year US sales: 43,411 240 and leftover 140 sales in 1975, 33,582 240s in 1976, 46,727 in 1980 and 58,945 in 1981, per Curbside Classic's multi-part history drawing on Volvo's period sales reports. Two variant counts matter to collectors. Wikipedia states that Volvo built 505 evolution versions of the 240 Turbo in 1983 for Group A homologation, all exported to the United States, with 270 of them retrofitted with the special equipment at Long Beach; Hagerty's buyer's guide describes an auction car claimed to be one of 500 flat-hood cars. The 500 versus 505 difference is not resolved by any source consulted here. For the final 1993 Classic edition, Wikipedia states that only 1,600 were produced for North America in April and May 1993; no second source for that number was found.",
